00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:31.740
The news is our connection to the world.&nbsp;
Whether you wake up and listen to a podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:38.520
or grab a morning paper, journalists are&nbsp;
responsible for the information you use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.260
form an accurate picture of the world. 
It’s an easy thing to take for granted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:50.400
But as authoritarian populism surges worldwide,&nbsp;
journalism has become an increasingly dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.340
profession - and populations are losing their&nbsp;
access to the news they need. Nowhere is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.740
trend more dramatic than in Latin America,&nbsp;
especially in our closest neighbor Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.840
which has become one of the most dangerous&nbsp;
places on Earth for reporting the news. 
&nbsp;

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:13.980
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters.&nbsp;
Today, vanishing press freedom in Latin America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:20.460
and the global assault on the news. 
Gabrielle SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:29.040
What do you think is a healthy relationship&nbsp;
between the government and journalism? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.920
it feels like there's this natural antagonism&nbsp;
there, but when does it get unhealthy?
&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.940
Carin ZISSIS: It gets unhealthy when we start&nbsp;
to see people in high levels of government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.940
attack journalists verbally. Because if&nbsp;
people at very high levels of government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:54.900
start criticizing the press, calling into&nbsp;
question journalists and journalism, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.420
we find is that then that trickles down to other&nbsp;
levels of government and it becomes the norm.
&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:08.820
SIERRA: This is Carin Zissis, editor-in-chief of&nbsp;
AS/COA Online, the website of the Americas Society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.640
and Council of the Americas. She is also a veteran&nbsp;
reporter on Mexican and Latin American affairs. 
&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:22.140
ZISSIS: It starts to become the norm to doubt,&nbsp;
question, criticize, attack journalists, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:28.620
that leads to actual violence against journalists.&nbsp;
Then that becomes part of authoritarian control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.660
because you're preventing a questioning of what&nbsp;
the government is doing. It's really a starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.680
point for democratic decay.
Jan-Albert HOOTSEN:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.780
I do believe that both in Mexico and&nbsp;
beyond on a global level journalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:51.120
is becoming more dangerous.
 
SIERRA: This is Jan-Albert Hootsen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.620
a veteran reporter who is also the Mexico&nbsp;
representative for the Committee to Protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.080
Journalists. This means he monitors press freedom&nbsp;
in the region and helps reporters at risk.
&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:08.400
HOOTSEN: There is a tendency globally&nbsp;
to increasingly target anybody who might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.760
be involved in making information publicly&nbsp;
available that will touch on the interests of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.620
very powerful players. They might be state.&nbsp;
They might be non-state. But in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.780
the journalists and human rights defenders are&nbsp;
becoming more legitimate targets. We're also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:30.900
dealing with the reality of changing technologies&nbsp;
and increased surveillance, often illegal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.120
of reporters. And I think that also makes it more&nbsp;
dangerous, both for journalists and for sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:46.680
I think what we're seeing is the very logical, if&nbsp;
not unfortunate, consequence of a media landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:54.180
that has been changing at a very staggering&nbsp;
rate. Over the past, say, twenty to thirty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:01.320
years with the arrival of the internet, with the&nbsp;
arrival of social media, with the arrival of a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:08.520
kind of populism amongst politicians that largely&nbsp;
derives much of its appeal from these new ways of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:16.380
communication. And it has exposed journalism to&nbsp;
a new kind of scrutiny that didn't exist before.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.120
SIERRA: In the case of Latin America, some&nbsp;
of this scrutiny has been put into writing. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.660
spate of recent laws have been aimed at silencing&nbsp;
reporters. In 2021, Nicaraguan President Daniel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:37.140
Ortega approved a law forcing media outlets&nbsp;
to register as “foreign agents,” giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.600
government near-total oversight of their spending&nbsp;
and political activities. That same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.540
a sweeping bill was passed in Ecuador that&nbsp;
criminalized the act of publishing digital content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.180
about a person without their consent. Changes&nbsp;
like these target some of the basic tools that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.580
journalists need in order to do their job. 
HOOTSEN:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:07.320
Mexico has always been an extremely difficult&nbsp;
country for the press. The country was ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:14.220
for approximately 70 years as a one-party state.&nbsp;
So for the press during those 70 years in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:19.290
twentieth century, when the modern press as we&nbsp;
know it was largely shaped and created, the press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:25.980
in Mexico had to make a choice: either they became&nbsp;
part of that political complex, meaning that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:32.220
would become basically a mouthpiece of the party&nbsp;
in power, or they would become independent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:40.140
by being free media they would be exposed to all&nbsp;
kinds of threats and pressures. As Mexico became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:46.320
democracy or at least a partial democracy in 2000.&nbsp;
There was an explosion in independent journalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:52.200
and independent media which was unfortunately soon&nbsp;
caught up by two realities. One of them being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:59.100
economic reality. Mexico is a country where it is&nbsp;
extremely difficult for a news outlet to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.960
because if you're independent, you're not going to&nbsp;
get a lot of government advertisement. The other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.600
aspect which makes journalism increasingly&nbsp;
difficult is the extreme violence against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:18.960
reporters which started rising in the 1990s and&nbsp;
really exploded when Mexico informally launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.220
the “war on drugs” which is very notorious&nbsp;
on an international level right now. 
&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:29.160
SIERRA: By the numbers, Latin America is&nbsp;
consistently ranked as one of the deadliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:35.640
regions in the world for reporters, with 30&nbsp;
journalists killed just last year. And within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:43.920
Latin America, Mexico is the most dangerous&nbsp;
country of all. In 2021, 664 attacks were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:50.700
documented against the press there. And in 2022,&nbsp;
nearly 40 percent of journalists killed in Latin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:56.520
America died in Mexico. Their deaths have been&nbsp;
attributed to a mix of gang violence, political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:03.120
corruption, and a lack of arrests and prosecution&nbsp;
for dangerous criminals. Only Ukraine - a country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.560
in the middle of a desperate war - saw more&nbsp;
journalist deaths than Mexico in 2022.
&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.140
Al Jazeera English: Over the past few decades&nbsp;
the country’s violence and corruption has meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:16.920
journalists face attacks from everywhere.
CBS News: 2022 has been one of the deadliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.840
years ever for journalists in Mexico.
Al Jazeera: Intimidation, imprisonment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.080
harassment, and assassinations.
 
ZISSIS: In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.180
spite of being a democracy, in spite of&nbsp;
being a country that's not currently at war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:38.220
it is the most dangerous country in the world for&nbsp;
journalists. If we really look at the numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:42.780
this increase in violence against&nbsp;
journalists began with the start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.900
of Mexico's offensive on cartels. Since that&nbsp;
point, more than 125 journalists have died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:57.840
There was a very important election in 2018&nbsp;
in which Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador won the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.520
presidency, and he came in with this huge&nbsp;
mandate. He won by a landslide and he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:09.720
sustained popularity over time. And at the&nbsp;
same time it's coincided with a questioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:17.340
of democratic institutions. So the press&nbsp;
being a key example of that. But we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:23.520
have an issue of what's happening in addition&nbsp;
around, for example, the electoral agency in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:30.060
Mexico. The current government is trying to find&nbsp;
ways to decrease its funding and to weaken some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:35.940
aspects of the INE, the electoral agency here.&nbsp;
Just like the United States, Mexico's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:44.100
have another presidential election in 2024. 
SIERRA: In 2022, President Andres Manuel Lopez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:49.860
Obrador aka AMLO, threatened members of the&nbsp;
Supreme Court, reduced checks and balances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:54.420
and tightened his grip on political&nbsp;
power. That same year, violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.880
against journalists hit an all time high. 
According to the Economist’s Democracy Index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:05.880
report, Mexico is now considered a ‘hybrid&nbsp;
regime,’ walking the line between a flawed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:11.220
democracy and a full-fledged authoritarian&nbsp;
state. And after the past four and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.720
years of extreme press censorship and&nbsp;
backsliding, the outlook for Mexican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:21.660
journalists is growing more and more grim. 
ZISSIS: Now the problem keeps getting worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:28.500
2022 was the deadliest year on record. And&nbsp;
we've seen this rise in violence against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:34.620
journalists since the start of the Lopez Obrador&nbsp;
administration. It's been over time. It's to such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:40.200
a degree that the president starts every day&nbsp;
with a press conference that goes on for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:46.680
couple of hours. It allows him to set the agenda&nbsp;
for what people are going to talk about in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:52.260
country. And there is a segment in his, what we&nbsp;
call the mañanera, the daily press conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:59.340
called “Quién es Quién en las Mentiras,” it's the&nbsp;
who's who in the lying, who's who of the liars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:06.180
And during the segment in his press conference,&nbsp;
they actually put on a big screen pictures of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:14.280
columnists, journalists, critics of the government&nbsp;
and try to discredit their comments and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.600
reporting or their coverage or their columns,&nbsp;
talking about things that are going on.
&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:27.586
El Universal: “Quién es quién en las mentiras de&nbsp;
la semana,” así se va a llamar. “Quién es quién en&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.586 --> 00:10:31.260
las mentiras de la semana.”
ZISSIS:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:37.200
It's almost like a kind of blacklisting.&nbsp;
What then happens is these same journalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.660
then start to receive attacks. They start to&nbsp;
receive attacks online. Really frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:46.020
attacks. Sometimes they get followed.&nbsp;
There have been all kinds of stories. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:54.900
it's really quite scary. In December there was&nbsp;
an attack on a very high profile journalist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:59.700
Mexico named Ciro Gómez Leyva. When it&nbsp;
happens to someone who is so well known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.720
such a prominent journalist. It really sent&nbsp;
a message to journalists across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:09.960
this is what can happen to you. And he was someone&nbsp;
who had been under attack by the AMLO government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:15.600
And in fact after the attack, AMLO suggested that&nbsp;
maybe the attack was carried out just to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:21.300
the government look bad, as though it were a fake&nbsp;
attack. So, it creates these narratives of what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:27.060
true and what's false. Let's blur the lines, let's&nbsp;
make it confusing. So, it really can create this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:33.060
atmosphere where this type of violence occurs.
SIERRA: Populists and authoritarian leaders are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:38.940
natural enemies of the press. And in order to&nbsp;
consolidate power, most of these leaders feel they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:43.500
have to crack down on journalists. It’s something&nbsp;
that is playing out all over the world. 
&nbsp;

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:49.500
For example, since his term began in 2010,&nbsp;
Hungary’s Prime Minister, Viktor Orbán,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:54.600
has effectively dismantled press freedom.&nbsp;
President Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:59.280
has also shaped the news to his advantage -&nbsp;
his country has one of the poorest ratings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:04.080
for press freedom as well as some of the highest&nbsp;
numbers of incarcerated journalists in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:10.980
And in the Philippines, former President Rodrigo&nbsp;
Duterte threatened journalists during his rule by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:17.520
saying they’re ‘not exempt from assassination.’
The playbook goes all the way back to the fascist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:23.580
leaders of the 20th century, and even earlier.&nbsp;
Lopez Obrador, for all his individual quirks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:29.820
is part of a long and dark tradition. 
ZISSIS: I think a really important reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:34.800
that we have to pay attention to what's happening&nbsp;
in Mexico is because it serves as a warning. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:40.140
serves as a warning in terms of what happens&nbsp;
if there are attacks on the press. Certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:46.080
Mexico is a younger democracy. It has weaker&nbsp;
institutions, and it shows really what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:53.100
when you do weaken those institutions, when you do&nbsp;
have an erosion of institutions. And when we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:59.400
the violence in a country just across the border&nbsp;
having record numbers of journalists getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:05.940
killed at a time of criticism of the press, at&nbsp;
this time of eroding protections for the press,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:11.760
it's something that we can't just ignore. It's a&nbsp;
warning of what will happen if we do. And freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:17.580
of the press is a key part of democracy.
SIERRA: So, you say it’s a warning - are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:23.220
seeing similar dynamics unfold here in the U.S.?
ZISSIS: I think something that we've seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:30.240
recent years in the United States is a questioning&nbsp;
of the press. We hear language around “the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:36.540
mainstream media does this” or “only covers that.”&nbsp;
And we've seen high level government officials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:42.360
including even, obviously, President Trump,&nbsp;
be very vocal in attacking the press and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:46.680
coming after journalists. And really&nbsp;
kind of vilifying the press in a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:52.560
way. Some of the language that we have heard&nbsp;
is really reminiscent of the type of threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:59.280
you hear against the press in other countries.
SIERRA: Okay, so we are seeing some international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:05.220
themes here, but zooming back in on Mexico,&nbsp;
is there anything unique that we’re seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.660
there? Are these dynamics different than what&nbsp;
you’re seeing in the rest of Latin America? 
&nbsp;

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:16.200
ZISSIS: Mexico has some of the same challenges&nbsp;
we see in other parts of Latin America in that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:21.900
because there has been this history, particularly&nbsp;
when Mexico wasn't really a democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:27.300
of journalists sometimes getting paid off,&nbsp;
and a system in which journalists were in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:33.180
way part of that system of maintaining this semi&nbsp;
authoritarian situation. We have similarities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:38.040
other parts of Latin America, but the problem&nbsp;
with the fact that that was in the past is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:44.520
the current government now tries to recall that&nbsp;
memory. There's actually a word for it in Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:50.760
you're called a “chayotero,” that's a journalist&nbsp;
who is thought to be bought off by officials or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:56.280
bought off in some way, bribed in some way&nbsp;
in terms of its coverage. I think what we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:03.780
seeing is a general increase in attacks in Latin&nbsp;
America and murders. And unfortunately that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:09.360
this model that we've seen in Mexico of attacks on&nbsp;
the press, verbal attacks on press, it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.560
that can carry over into other countries and&nbsp;
that has carried over into other countries.
&nbsp;

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:18.445
HOOTSEN: Everything that is happening in&nbsp;
Mexico nowadays, whether it's economical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.445 --> 00:15:22.800
whether it's to do with drug violence&nbsp;
or whether it's to do with politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:28.380
is in one way or another connected to the outside&nbsp;
world. So press freedom is the same thing. Press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:36.480
freedom in a single country never just exists&nbsp;
in a vacuum. It's part of a regional or even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:40.620
global development. You know, we've talked about&nbsp;
populism. We've talked about the way politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:46.080
are dealing with politicians everywhere around&nbsp;
the globe, they will be copying each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:52.260
I think a good example here in Latin America&nbsp;
is Nayib Bukele, the president of El Salvador,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:58.020
extremely popular, also extremely authoritarian&nbsp;
president who is democratically elected but is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:01.920
trying to erode the democracy in his country.
CBS Evening News: The country with the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.929
murder rate in the world for young&nbsp;
people under the age of 19.
&nbsp;

00:16:05.929 --> 00:16:10.560
Vice News: The whole question of public safety&nbsp;
