own little Salvadoran variety of it. So none of&nbsp;
this ever exists in the void. And that's why it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:18.060
really important for everybody to understand&nbsp;
that what's happening in your country will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:22.500
happening in other countries. Politicians, the&nbsp;
public, media - we're all looking at each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:26.520
We’re all copying things from each other and&nbsp;
that's why it's important to understand both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:32.100
the local context and the global context.
Neil BRANDVOLD: It's a very scary time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:36.240
right now in El Salvador. 
SIERRA: This is Neil Brandvold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:40.500
He’s an American documentary filmmaker and&nbsp;
a photojournalist based in El Salvador.&nbsp;&nbsp;

