00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:39.900
repercussions from the government. So when we go&nbsp;
out to report and do stories, our access is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:45.180
limited because people are afraid to speak out&nbsp;
against the government on TV. So if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:48.840
have an idea of how bad things have gotten here&nbsp;
in El Salvador, the journalists that have fled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:53.160
the majority of them have fled to Mexico, which&nbsp;
has historically been one of the most dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:58.440
places to be a journalist. And will be reporting&nbsp;
on El Salvador from Mexico because they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:03.780
afraid of repercussions from the government.
SIERRA: Bukele enjoys enormous popularity, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:09.540
tries to strike the image of a young, hip leader&nbsp;
- wearing leather jackets and baseball caps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:16.080
and wielding a savvy command of social media. In&nbsp;
fact, Bukele enjoys 4 million Instagram followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:22.260
and 5 million twitter followers, and his habit&nbsp;
of attacking critics online has earned him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:29.220
moniker “the millennial dictator.” 
BRANDVOLD: He doesn't allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

