00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:34.620
in El Salvador have dropped 56 percent. The&nbsp;
change reinforced his popularity enormously.
&nbsp;

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:40.560
Critics, however, note that in the process, El&nbsp;
Salvador attained the highest incarceration rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:47.160
in the world, with about 2 percent of its adult&nbsp;
population living behind bars. Human rights groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:51.960
have raised concerns about wrongful arrests&nbsp;
and abusive conditions in prison facilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:56.400
but these criticisms have done nothing&nbsp;
to jeopardize Bukele’s popularity.
&nbsp;

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:02.160
BRANDVOLD: It's really interesting. I think a lot&nbsp;
of people internationally would never grasp how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:08.040
bad the situation under gangs were in El Salvador.&nbsp;
It was incredibly violent. The highest homicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:11.880
rate in the entire world outside of Syria for&nbsp;
years. They were just incredibly violent. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.680
people that live there just suffered horribly.&nbsp;
I covered the crime scene in homicide beat for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:21.240
years, going out at night and we would be bouncing&nbsp;
from murder, to murder, to murder. Horrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:26.520
horrible murders. And the general public, when&nbsp;
he came in and he said, "I'm going to dismantle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:31.560
the gangs. I don't care about human rights. Throw&nbsp;
everyone in the prison." In the last 10 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:37.620
he's arrested 60,000 people. 60,000 people that&nbsp;
have no access to a lawyer, to see a judge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:44.100
or any habeas corpus. And the general population I&nbsp;
think was so fed up with living under the fist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:48.060
these really violent, horrible gang members that&nbsp;
would extort them, would murder them, would kidnap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:52.800
their kids, that they're just like, "Let him do&nbsp;
what he is doing." And you speak to people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.240
they're like, "He's dismantled the gangs," which&nbsp;
is largely true. For the most part, the gangs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:02.700
are not operating like they used to. So I think&nbsp;
your average person says, "Keep going, Bukele."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:08.280
He's illegally, unconstitutionally running for a&nbsp;
reelection, he's announced, and will probably win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:14.160
by a very large majority. And it's been kind of&nbsp;
this handoff of security and democracy. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:18.840
look at democracy in El Salvador, it’s this very&nbsp;
short-lived democracy. The Civil War ended in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:23.700
'90s. We're talking like 30 years of democracy&nbsp;
- that's been a lot of failed governments. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:28.200
party has been super corrupt. And they're just&nbsp;
looking at this, "We want security." So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:35.100
just shut us off or attack us in the streets.&nbsp;
It's made it very difficult to feel like you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:38.280
making a change because the average person in&nbsp;
El Salvador just doesn't want to hear it.
&nbsp;

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:45.060
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit more&nbsp;
about how Bukele has made it hard for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:51.540
and for other journalists to report the news?
BRANDVOLD: Access used to be very open prior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:56.160
Bukele. I mean, we were spending months in the&nbsp;
prisons. I had access to the forensics teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:00.720
which is really important here to be able&nbsp;
to get really good numbers on homicides and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:06.480
anything like that. Before Bukele was president&nbsp;
I interviewed him in his campaign many times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:10.740
it was very open. As journalists, we all knew&nbsp;
that things were going to change when he came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:15.360
to power. And they changed really quickly.&nbsp;
It went from pretty much open access where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:19.260
you could go with a forensics team, or ride&nbsp;
along with the police, or go into the jails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:25.500
to absolutely no information, no access. And then,&nbsp;
it spiraled into we found that journalists' phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:29.880
were all hacked. We were getting Pegasus. Over&nbsp;
30 journalists here were infected with Pegasus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:33.777
And that's just a small amount of the spyware&nbsp;
they've been using against journalists.
&nbsp;

00:23:33.777 --> 00:23:39.600
Al Jazeera English: Pegasus has been used by&nbsp;
governments around the world to spy on dissidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:43.620
and journalists via their mobile phones.
60 Minutes: We look specifically at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:50.640
controversial Israeli called the NSO Group that&nbsp;
says it developed a hacking tool that can break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:54.660
into just about any smartphone on Earth.
Al Jazeera English: What’s particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:58.380
concerning about this spyware is, unlike&nbsp;
most infections where you have to click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:00.900
on something or do something,&nbsp;
it just gets into your phone.
&nbsp;

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:08.160
SIERRA: Journalists have always worried about&nbsp;
being spied on, but the threat of invasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:14.400
spyware is unique to the current generation. Long&nbsp;
story short, once a phone has been compromised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:20.700
Pegasus, everything on it can be viewed, including&nbsp;
calls, texts, location, and recorded materials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:26.160
It’s a perfect tool for would-be authoritarians.
BRANDVOLD: So, we know that El Salvador purchased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:30.360
it from this Israeli company, Pegasus. They&nbsp;
have access to basically everything on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:34.500
phone. So they know where you're going, and they&nbsp;
