00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:39.525
photos, your contacts, your microphone, your&nbsp;
camera, everything. They have full access.
&nbsp;

00:24:39.525 --> 00:24:42.660
SIERRA: That's pretty scary. Yeah.
BRANDVOLD: Yeah. Very scary. But speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:48.300
with contacts in the US State Department, we know&nbsp;
that that's just a small portion of the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:54.000
spyware and hacking abilities that the government&nbsp;
is capable of. I mean, the amount of security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:58.800
measures that we have to take as journalists in&nbsp;
El Salvador, has just - it's skyrocketed. Using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:03.660
different apps to scan our phones to see if it's&nbsp;
infected. But then also knowing most likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:09.300
government has access to everything on our phone&nbsp;
and just communicating in a way with the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:13.440
the government's listening. 
HOOTSEN: Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:18.000
thing that's very important to point out here&nbsp;
is that Pegasus is only one kind of spyware.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:24.120
It's only one of dozens that we know are being&nbsp;
used by governments across the globe. We're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:29.940
nearly done investigating Pegasus, and, in the&nbsp;
meantime, how many other kinds of spyware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:35.580
are much harder to detect may have already been&nbsp;
bought by the Mexican government and by other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:39.480
governments in Latin America? 
SIERRA: As technology evolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:45.600
the risk for journalists gets even greater.&nbsp;
Hostile rhetoric circulating online can easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:51.060
translate into very real violence amongst the&nbsp;
public. And as some leaders in Latin America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:58.260
further limit access to independent information,&nbsp;
citizens are being led astray by propaganda that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:03.420
dictates how they should view the press. 
This is certainly the case in Mexico and El&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:08.520
Salvador, and echoes of the problem can be&nbsp;
heard from Eastern Europe to South Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:15.480
and even in the United States. A strong free&nbsp;
press is, after all, a hard thing to build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:22.020
and an easy thing to lose, and it is an eternal&nbsp;
thorn in the side of those who would establish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:28.680
a monopoly on power. Even now, despite these&nbsp;
global setbacks, journalists are still reporting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:34.740
finding new ways to circumvent obstacles, and&nbsp;
hoping that soon they may be able to do their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:39.540
jobs without a target on their backs.
ZISSIS: I have to say that it's incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

